data.content:
# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `ThreadAbortException` | **Never raised** |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Removed** |
| Manual `Thread` + `volatile bool` | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces ThreadAbortException handling
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        // Signal cancellation (replaces _running = false)
        await _cts.CancelAsync();

        // Wait with timeout (replaces Thread.Sleep + Abort)
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)))
        {
            // Task didn't finish in time — log it, but you can't
            // force-kill it. This is by design in .NET 8.
            Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

**1. You cannot force-kill work in .NET 8.** There is no replacement
for `Thread.Abort()`. Your blocking calls (`GetNextItem`) **must**
accept a `CancellationToken` — otherwise `StopAsync` will hang:

```csharp
// ❌ Old: blocks forever, relied on Thread.Abort to escape
var item = queue.Take();

// ✅ New: throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
var item = await queue.TakeAsync(token);
// — or for sync APIs: —
var item = blockingCollection.Take(token);
```

**2. `catch (ThreadAbortException)` silently becomes dead code.** The
compiler won't warn you. The rollback logic will **never execute**
unless you replace it with `OperationCanceledException`.

**3. `Thread.ResetAbort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** at
runtime — not a compile error. Search your codebase:

```bash
grep -rn "ResetAbort\|Thread\.Abort\|ThreadAbortException" --include="*.cs"
```

**4. `volatile bool` is insufficient for async.** A `CancellationToken`
is checked cooperatively by async APIs (`Task.Delay`, `Channel`,
`SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync`, etc.). A bare `volatile bool` can't wake
a blocked `await`.

**5. Fire-and-forget `Task.Run` loses exceptions.** Always store the
`Task` and observe it in `StopAsync`/`DisposeAsync` to surface errors.

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Cancellable

As a last resort, you can isolate truly uncancellable work on a
dedicated thread and abandon it:

```csharp
// Escape hatch — not recommended, leaks the thread
private void ProcessLoopSync(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        var item = GetNextItem(); // legacy, can't change
        if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            break;
        }
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
}
```

This thread will **not** be killed on cancellation — it will only stop
at the next loop iteration. Accept this or refactor the blocking API.
